Well, this is what happens when I get laid off.  I'm the author of the
original telnetTiles app (specialized telnet client for nethack
servers that replaces the ansi display with the standard tile set,
enables a scrollback message buffer, allows the use the mouse for
travel and prompts, and enables the alt key commands).  In my (now
more copious) free time, I've expanded my skill set, and in the course
of teaching myself Java, I rewrote telnetTiles as an applet, and
started my own patched server to beta test it.

Here's what I've got so far:

   1) it *is* indeed a telnet client, it talks enough of the telnet
protocol to link up successfully to my unix implementation of telnetd
serving just nethack, and it knows enough ANSI to deal with rendering
nethack.

   2) it has the tiles up, and with the original telnetTiles patch it
doesn't need to guess (which is good, cause I haven't reimplemented
       that yet)

   3) it's got the message window and the stats bar, the message
window has at least one known bug (when you are at a typing
       prompt, it repeats the entire line of text for every character
you type or delete)  The stats bar is minimally implemented, I'm
       looking for ideas for a more aesthetically pleasing way to
present that information.

   4)  it does the alt commands

   5)  it does the on screen menu to dialog box, but the dialog box is
not yet capable of dealing with mouse clicks.  All it does is
        pass thru the key clicks to the underlying nethack
connection.  I'm still wrestling with how I want it to look, and how I
want it
        it to deal with partial pick up and drops (ex ",20a" to pick
up 20 of the first item in the list, how should I deal with that
'20'?)

Now that it's resurrected, I've got new energy to do cool things with
it... for instance, once I've gotten the last few bits I've mentioned
above, I've built in hooks for dealing with alternate tilesets!  The
old one never had that, as it's set was compiled in as a resource.

